{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg8pAnaG21QFe87QQvifP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"[x] Announce times, corrupt-peer counters and stall samples are forgotten from the torrent's own close handler, not only from stop-torrenting (source scan)\n[x] A torrent that closed and is started again under the same info hash does not report as stalled on its first progress update\n\nThe engine now forgets a torrent's announce time, corrupt-peer count and stall samples whenever the torrent closes: stop, removal or a fatal error. So a resumed torrent starts with a clean stall clock. The full `npm test` passes (4245/4245, lint included), and it's committed as `1ff21a3`.\n\n**What changed**\n1. `src/engine/index.js`: `addTorrentEvents` now sets one close handler, `torrentClose`, which does all three cleanups. WebTorrent fires `close` from `destroy()` whatever the cause, so `stopTorrenting` now just destroys the torrent and no longer repeats the cleanup. Tickets 03 and 05 can add their own cleanup to the same handler.\n2. I added a guard that the ticket didn't ask for. When a torrent is added twice, WebTorrent destroys the second copy, and that copy fires `close` under the same info hash. Without a check, it would wipe the running torrent's state. A closing torrent is taken off the client before `close` fires, so the handler returns early if `client.get(infoHash)` still finds one.\n3. New test `test/unit/engine-torrent-close-wiring.js` scans the source, like the existing wiring tests. It checks that there is exactly one close handler, that it clears all three kinds of state, that the duplicate guard comes first, and that `stopTorrenting` keeps no copy of the cleanup.\n4. `src/engine/AGENTS.md` has a new section on the close handler. I removed the old paragraph saying fatal-error leftovers were harmless.\n\n**The second checklist item has no end-to-end test.** No new test checks that a resumed torrent isn't marked stalled on its first progress update. That would mean waiting through a real 60-second stall and forcing a fatal torrent error from the running engine. Instead, it rests on two tests together: the existing \"a forgotten torrent starts over\" test in `engine-stall.js` shows the stall clock restarts once forgotten, and the new scan shows the close handler is what forgets it. Both items went in one commit because they come from the same code change. This is written down in `.nightralph/notes.md`, which is left uncommitted as instructed."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":402,"cache_read_input_tokens":76200,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":402},"output_tokens":696,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"88e1bba6-f1bf-43ef-aa70-44826bcced76","uuid":"46c6ea43-9ada-4476-aa35-844cf7ee9051","timestamp":"2026-10-18T01:12:43.510Z","request_id":"req_011Cg8pAnJeB4uq9yrevaUdE"}
